\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Capital curbs,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  stifle Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  market  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao  \par \uc2 866 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2006\par 05:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nov 30 (Reuters) - Corporate demand for deep, liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is rising in Asia, but such thirst will hardly be slaked unless governments relax capital controls and resist the urge to control exchange rates through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The corporate pressure is adding to the political pressure from western nations for Asian governments to relax capital controls and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to help resolve global economic imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 However, few analysts expect change to occur quickly even as the International Monetary Fund adopts a new role of international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policeman.  \par \par \uc2 Huw McKay, senior economist at Westpac Bank, believes more Asian countries will liberalise capital accounts as they benefit from strong growth and improving banking systems, but change will be gradual.  \par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market would be a clear winner, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Turnover in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will be a high beta respondent to liberalisation of Asia's international financial arrangements," he wrote in a recent research report.  \par \par \uc2 His figures show trade in the emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Asia is just a fraction of the region's contribution to global output, underlining the depth of capital controls.  \par \par \uc2 But equally, Asia runs large trade and current account surpluses, which regional exporters want to hedge.  \par \par \uc2 Daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  turnover in seven countries -- China, Thailand, the Philippines, Indonesia, Malaysia, Taiwan, South Korea -- currently amounts to just $36 billion, McKay says, based on data from the Bank for International Settlements.  \par \par \uc2 That is less than 2 percent of turnover in the $2-trillion-per-day global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, yet the region generates 26 percent of world economic output.  \par \par \uc2 "Pent-up demand for freer access to deeper \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in Asia is in evidence. That predicts a rapid rate of catch-up for turnover once the 'supply side' policy adjustment process begins in earnest," McKay said, referring to capital account liberalisation.  \par \par \uc2 Illustrating the demand for access to more open markets, Chinese banks were active participants in the offshore yuan non-deliverable forwards market until the government imposed a ban in October because it wants to develop its domestic markets.  \par \par \uc2 GRADUAL REFORMS  \par \par \uc2 China revalued the yuan by 2.1 percent in July 2005 and has since introduced a string of steps to help firms hedge risk, but the markets are small, limiting the volume of trade.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea and Taiwan have relaxed capital controls. The latest major policy initiative has come from India with a five-year plan for fuller capital account convertibility.  \par \par \uc2 China has pledged to make the yuan <CNY=CFXS> fully convertible, though it has yet to commit to a firm timetable.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, most Asian countries have yet to dismantle capital curbs imposed in the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis to deter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators, although policy makers have shifted the focus to restricting capital inflows, rather than capital flight.  \par \par \uc2 Asian countries are reluctant to relax capital flows partly because of their lopsided economies that rely on exports for growth in the absence of strong domestic consumption.  \par \par \uc2 That has left \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure to appreciate as consistent trade surpluses have produced around $3 trillion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves across Asia, including Japan's holdings.  \par \par \uc2 It has also drawn criticism from the West, led by the United States, who want Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise at a faster pace to help reduce trade imbalances.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 But Asian governments fear that allowing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise too fast could hurt exports and weaken their economies, so they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2 In Thailand, where the baht <THB=> has risen 14 percent so far this year against the dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Tarisa Watanagase said on Wednesday that the bank had acted to slow the baht's rise and would keep monitoring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Dealers estimate South Korean authorities spent $1 billion on Wednesday to curb the won's <KRW=> strength. It wasn't the first time the government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.  \par \par \uc2 "Traditionally, Asian countries always want to maintain stable exchange rates. Whenever there is a surge in volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , authorities step in and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Wang Qing, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America.  \par \par \uc2 "Little volatility can be expected if governments don't want to nurture \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and there is little demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives if there is no volatility," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Tim Condon, economist at ING, said it would take time for Asian countries to remove their capital restrictions due partly to their less developed capital markets, despite the changing economic landscape since the Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it's just a matter of capital market development and they will get around with it eventually, but they have to get over the strong desire to limit fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say any shift in policies would hinge on Asian countries' willingness to change their export-led growth model.  \par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, any shift in policies hinge on whether the countries are willing to change their export-led growth model. But but right now, they haven't fully embraced such changes," said Wang at Bank of America.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|Z|PHD|SI|IF|SNI  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061203e2bu0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
