\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia edges down as stocks, oil weigh  \b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2006\par 03:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 28 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged down against the dollar on Tuesday, undermined by a sell-off in equities, a rise in oil prices and wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. equities fell sharply on Monday, hurt by doubts over holiday spending after a disappointing sales estimate from Wal-Mart Stores Inc. . A 1.36 percent fall in the broad S&P 500 index <.SPX> was the biggest since early June.  \par \par \uc2 Latin American markets, tracking Wall Street, also suffered heavy losses with the Brazilian real <BRBY> falling 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 This in turn hurt Asia's high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> and Philippine peso <PHP=>, and led to their underperformance within the region.  \par \par \uc2 "Obviously the overnight equity market losses have restrained Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and introduced two-way risk into the market," said Claudio Piron, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah shed almost 0.6 percent to 9,188 per dollar, its weakest in nearly two weeks, and the peso fell to 49.92 per dollar -- down 0.6 percent from Monday's 4-1/2-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 "The tension in Latin American markets yesterday and the sharp fall in the Dow Jones is spilling over," said a Manila trader.  \par \par \uc2 Asian shares were broadly lower, with the MSCI's broadest index of shares <.MSCIAPJ> in the region excluding Japan, down about 1.6 percent by 0801 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices, meanwhile, extended gains above $60 a barrel <CLc1>, weighing on local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts pointed to a fall in the Chicago Board Options Exchange Volatility Index <.VIX>, or VIX, as a bearish signal for regional markets.  \par \par \uc2 The VIX, often called Wall Street's fear gauge, on Monday jumped more than 13 percent to 12.23 in its biggest one-day percentage surge since mid-July when record oil prices were pushing stock prices around.  \par \par \uc2 "The VIX index has broken to new levels, so the external environment suggests Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation may encounter some bumps," said Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Christy Tan.  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan's Piron said U.S. economic data and a speech by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke on the economic outlook later on Tuesday could provide markets with fresh direction.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the South Korean won <KRW=> hovered around 930.70 per dollar, off the previous day's nine-year highs, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> was up a touch at about 32.65 to the U.S. dollar -- but down about 0.4 percent from three-month peaks, touched in early trade.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> was quoted at about 1.5518 to the U.S. dollar, little changed from levels traded late in Asia on Monday, and off a nine-year peak struck last week at 1.5467.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is calm but I think it is wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar trader. "But the trend is bearish U.S. dollar."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0803 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan