\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr In Tight Range; Upside Capped By MAS Fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2006\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.5509   -0.0011 \par    Overnight rate        3.13%    -3bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  3.00%    -3bps \par    10-year bond yield:   3.03%    -2bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  3.43%    unch \par    10-year swap offer:   3.38%    -1bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   -5bps    -1bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)-- The Singapore dollar drifted slightly higher Tuesday as sentiment toward the U.S. dollar remained bearish, and the local unit is expected to remain in a tight range, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a regional bank said the market was "prone to US dollar selling," but added that traders were wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the US dollar falls below S$1.5500. S$1.5530 might provide resistance if the dollar strengthens, the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The trader said the US dollar seemed to be "retracing" its recent fall against the yen, and that "a spike in the dollar against the yen would push down the Singapore dollar," although such an outcome was still unlikely given that dollar sentiment remains bearish.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore bond market, yields on government bonds fell in light trade, following movements in US Treasury bonds overnight.  \par \par \uc2 A trader at a local bank expects activity to pick up during the rest of the week, following the release of key economic data in the US.  \par \par \uc2 During the U.S. trading day Tuesday, existing home sales are due at 1500 GMT and durable goods orders at 1330 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 "If those data move the market in the US, one would expect more trading activity tomorrow in Singapore bonds," the said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Aaron Back, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4157; aaron.back@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 28-11-06 0751GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061128e2bs000dd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
