\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  retreat on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 918 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2006\par 02:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nov 24 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell on Friday, giving up some of Thursday's stellar gains amid worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers suspected that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  played a hand in falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <SGD=> and the Indian rupee <INR=IN>, while South Korea reiterated its warnings against a rising won <KRW=>.  \par \par \uc2 Rhee Gwang-ju, head of the Bank of Korea's international department, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements.  \par \par \uc2 "It is more to do with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns and maybe a bit of position squaring," said Royal Bank of Scotland \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Ben Simpfendorfer, referring to the general weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar eased to 1.5581 per U.S. dollar, down about half a percent from Thursday's nine-year high at 1.5467, while the Indian rupee fell a similar amount to 44.95 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "They have been in pretty heavily today," said a trader in Singapore, referring to the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The won shed about a 0.25 percent to almost 933 per dollar, while the Philippine peso <PHP=> eased to 49.80 to the dollar -- down about a third of a percent from Thursday's 4-\u189\'00\'BD year high.  \par \par \uc2 Strong gains in the yen versus the dollar <JPY=> late in the Asian day helped some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trim losses, but most of them remained in negative territory.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders in Europe were focusing on reports of comments made by Wu Xiaoling, vice governor of the People's Bank of China, that the value of east Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were at risk from their exposure to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the comments had had little impact on Asian markets.  \par \par \uc2 Most regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had racked up firm gains on Thursday as position adjusting ahead of holidays in the United States and Japan triggered a broad sell-off in the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't think the move in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sustainable said Jimmy Koh, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at United Overseas Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "They will be driven by the majors and unless we see signs of Japanese interest rates rising, dollar/yen is unlikely to fall sharply."  \par \par \uc2 DIFFERING VIEWS Others, however, took a different view, saying there was scope for further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2 "Although the Fed is talking a bit more hawkishly, we've already got an eye on next year and potential BOJ tightening and yen strength," said Simpfendorfer at Royal Bank of Scotland.  \par \par \uc2 "With that in mind, you might expect markets to be looking for reasons to extend the Asian rally but there is not a huge amount of conviction there at the moment."  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> was little moved at about 36.55 per dollar, drawing some support from news that martial law in Thailand could be lifted in most areas by the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 General Sonthi Boonyaratglin, who led a military coup that overthrew Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra on Sept. 19, said in an interview with CNN television aired on Friday that he wanted a return to normal as soon as possible.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it is positive for the baht, as it is good to think that everything here is returning to a normal situation," said a Bangkok trader. "But the baht will still be driven by the capital flows and also the U.S. dollar trend."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers in Malaysia turned their attention to an interest rate decision from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to keep its overnight policy rate at 3.50 percent, especially after data this week showed Malaysia's consumer prices in October rose 3.1 percent from a year earlier -- the slowest increase since July 2005.  \par \par \uc2 "The case for an unchanged monetary stance was also supported by the lower-than-expected October inflation print this week," JPMorgan analysts said in a note. "We expect BNM (Bank Negara Malaysia) to remain on pause into 2007." \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0826 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.99         116.64       +0.56\par  Sing dlr        1.5540         1.5532       -0.05\par  Taiwan dlr      32.828         32.790       -0.12\par  Korean won      931.90         930.30       -0.17\par  Baht             36.53          36.53       +0.00\par  Peso             49.69          49.72       +0.06\par  Rupiah         9155.00        9126.00       -0.32\par  Rupee            44.79          44.70       -0.21\par  Ringgit         3.6370         3.6360       -0.03\par  Yuan            7.8547         7.8590       +0.05\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f