\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW: Singapore Oct CPI Contained; MAS Could Loosen  \b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2006\par 08:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's consumer price inflation stayed beneath the government's target range last month, which, analysts said, gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to adopt a looser policy in the event of an unwelcome economic slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 The consumer price index rose 0.4% from a year earlier, holding steady with the rate of increase in September, the government said Thursday. The rise was slightly higher than the average forecast of 0.3% in a Dow Jones Newswires poll.  \par \par \uc2 The very low level of inflation could signal a softer economy in the months ahead, but analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is well positioned to respond if the risk of deflation increases or output growth drops sharply.  \par \par \uc2 "If it looked like we were going down toward zero inflation, more accommodative policy could be an option," David Cohen, an economist at Action Economics, said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore advocates a modest and gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, and is believed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 It has a target range for inflation of 1% to 2% this year, and 0.5% to 1.5% next year.  \par \par \uc2 Though Cohen doesn't expect a policy shift to be "imminent", he said that a more accommodative policy would imply a Singapore dollar that holds steady versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They might say that (the Singapore dollar) should stay at a flat level," he said.  \par \par \uc2 DBS strategist Philip Wee said he expects the current policy to remain in place, but even a switch to looser policy wouldn't preclude further weakening of the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. dollar stays heavy, it could still decline (against the Singapore dollar), but the decline would be slower," Wee said.  \par \par \uc2 He estimates that the trade-weighted Singapore dollar is 0.9% above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undisclosed policy band midpoint.  \par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of this year, the Singapore dollar has appreciated about 7% against the U.S. dollar, which began the year around S$1.6620 and is now at S$1.5525.  \par \par \uc2 The stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped shield Singapore against external price pressures, the main source of inflation in the city-state.  \par \par \uc2 Among all sources of inflation, Cohen expects oil to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main focus in the months ahead.  \par \par \uc2 "A lot will depend on oil prices," he said.  \par \par \uc2 After rising to a high of around $78 in July, Nymex crude is now trading below $60 a barrel.  \par \par \uc2 In October, the consumer price index also rose 0.4% from September when adjusted for seasonal variations, just above a poll forecast of 0.3%.  \par \par \uc2 Lower fuel costs eased price pressures in the transport and communications sector. The inflation sub-index for the sector fell 1.9% from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 A possible sales tax increase next year could add to domestic price pressures, but Cohen still expects inflation to be tame.  \par \par \uc2 "The tax incrase could add maybe 0.5%...and the full-year rate would be 1.5% in 2007," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong recently announced plans to increase the tax on goods and services to 7% from 5% now.  \par \par \uc2 Among other key components, housing costs, which make up 21% of the index, gained 1.5% from a year earlier, and rose 0.7% from September due to higher electricity tariffs.  \par \par \uc2 The cost of food, which is the biggest component in the index with a 23% weighting, climbed 1.5% from a year earlier and 0.1% from September.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ven Ram [ 23-11-06 0730GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061123e2bn0018m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
