\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slip on yen,  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2006\par 04:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nov 15 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped on Wednesday due to persisting worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as the dollar edged up against the yen ahead of more U.S. data that could shape interest rate expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Still, many analysts expected Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume their advances against the dollar in the near future once the outlook for the U.S. economy and for rates is clearer.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks like the Asian central banks are getting their way right now, and now we're seeing the Thai baht looking vulnerable too after the Bank of Thailand said it's too volatile," ABN AMRO analyst Shahab Jalinoos said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 "We still think dollar/Asia is a longer-term sell, but for now it's better not to fight the contagion risk."  \par \par \uc2 Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Tarisa Watanagase said on Wednesday that the baht <THB=> is too volatile and the Bank of Thailand will smooth its volatility. The baht fell to 36.5 per dollar from Tuesday's highs around 36.36, its strongest in nearly seven years.  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been driven up by inflows of foreign portfolio investments and more recently by a Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment that Beijing planned to diversify its $1 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Fearful that stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could hurt exporters, central banks in South Korea, Thailand and India are suspected of having sold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  took their cue from the latest dollar/yen development, but I think they should strengthen over time," said Wang Qing, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 "Once the outlook of a U.S. soft-landing becomes more confirmed, I think Asian central banks should feel easier in handling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation," Wang said.  \par \par \uc2 STOCK RALLY  \par \par \uc2 Solid gains in Asian stocks, buoyed by capital inflows, would provide some support for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Asian stocks outside of Japan hit a record high on Wednesday following a rally on Wall Street. The MSCI index of Asian stocks outside Japan <.MSCIAPJ> hit a record high of 370.97 in early trade, before pulling back to around 370.54.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up against the yen <JPY=> on Wednesday after sliding on Tuesday on soft U.S. produce prices and retail sales figures that suggested a slowing economy.  \par \par \uc2 The United States releases consumer prices data on Thursday and housing starts figures on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> slipped for the third straight session on Wednesday, shedding nearly half a percent as investors covered dollar short positions. It briefly touched a session low of 942.1, the weakest since 943.4 on Nov. 7.  \par \par \uc2 But the Bank of Korea was not spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day, dealers said, having bought an estimated $1.5 billion over Monday and Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> shed nearly half a percent to 9,168 per dollar while the Indian rupee <INR=IN> fell a quarter of a percent against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> slipped around 0.20 percent to 1.5590 per U.S. dollar, moving further back from a nine-year high of 1.5545 that it struck late on Friday and matched overnight.  \par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYRX=> lost 0.26 percent against the dollar while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> fell 0.13 per to 32.81 per U.S. dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 Change on the day at 0831 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.75         117.52       -0.20\par  Sing dlr        1.5589         1.5555       -0.22\par  Taiwan dlr      32.808         32.765       -0.13\par  Korean won      941.30         936.90       -0.47\par  Baht             36.52          36.41       -0.30\par  Peso             50.07          49.91       -0.32\par  Rupiah         9175.00        9125.00       -0.54\par  Rupee            45.28          45.17       -0.23\par  Ringgit         3.6460         3.6365       -0.26\par  Yuan            7.8676         7.8669       -0.01\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       117.75         117.96       +0.18\par  Sing dlr        1.5589         1.6625       +6.65\par  Taiwan dlr      32.808         32.850       +0.13\par  Korean won      941.30        1011.60       +7.47\par  Baht             36.52          41.11      +12.57\par  Peso             50.07          53.09       +6.03\par  Rupiah         9175.00        9835.00       +7.19\par  Rupee            45.28          45.04       -0.52\par  Ringgit         3.6460         3.7790       +3.65\par  Yuan            7.8676         8.0702       +2.58\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\