\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down Slightly On Rumored MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2006\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD               1.5612   +0.0009 \par  Overnight Rate        3.13%    -25 bps \par  2 Year Bond Yield     3.06%    -2 bps \par  10-Year Bond Yield    3.15%    -1 bp \par  2-Year Swap Offer     3.49%    +1 bp \par  10-Year Swap Offer    3.53%    -1 bp \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve  4 bps    -2 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar fell in Asian trade Thursday on rumored \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dealers said a German bank likely acting on behalf of the MAS sold Singapore dollars aggressively from S$1.5625 in the morning, lifting the pair above S$1.5640.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS' key monetary policy tool is managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed trading band.  \par \par \uc2 "The pair should not go easily above S$1.5660 for the rest of the week," said one foreign bank dealer. "There is no reason to buy the pair."  \par \par \uc2 Another local bank dealer said there was downside risk for the pair.  \par \par \uc2 "I would expect the U.S. figures to be low tonight and the U.S. dollar may trade below SS1.5600 on the weak data," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The September U.S. trade deficit numbers are due for release at 1300 GMT. A Dow Jones newswires survey, however, forecasts the deficit to narrow from $69.86 billion in August to $65.8 billion in September on lower oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said any rise in the trade deficit would result in a greenback sell-off.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore bonds rose slightly, tracking a rise in U.S. Treasurys overnight.  \par \par \uc2 "Don't expect the bonds to change because of the U.S. trade deficit tonight," said a local dealer. "Traders should be more concerned about the U.S. October inflation numbers next week."  \par \par \uc2 If the U.S. consumer price index rises more than expected, bond prices will likely track U.S. Treasurys lower.  \par \par \uc2 -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires, 65-6415-4159, weiyi.lim@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 09-11-06 0607GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061109e2b9000w9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
