ghtly, tracking a rise in U.S. Treasurys overnight. "Don't expect the bonds to change because of the U.S. trade deficit tonight," said a local dealer. "Traders should be more concerned about the U.S. October inflation numbers next week." If the U.S. consumer price index rises more than expected, bond prices will likely track U.S. Treasurys lower. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires, 65-6415-4159, weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -Edited by Graham Norris -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020061109e2b9002p9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
