ar bonds on customers' purchases,' said a local bank dealer. 'With no new issuances locally, we can only track U.S. data,' he said. 'Expect volatility as there are a lot of U.S. data, starting tonight.' October's US Chicago Purchasing Managers Index will be released at 1500 GMT. If U.S. data is stronger than expected, bond prices will likely fall. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires, 65-6415-4118, weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -Edited by Graham Norris -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020061031e2av003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
