\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Gains On Asia Strength,Near Top Of MAS Band  \b0\par\par\uc2 450 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2006\par 10:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest   Change \par  USD/SGD               1.5663   -0.0018 \par  Overnight Rate        3.63%    +25 bps \par  2 Year Bond Yield     3.04%    +1 bp \par  10-Year Bond Yield    3.21%    -1 bp \par  2-Year Swap Offer     3.47%    -7 bp \par  10-Year Swap Offer    3.59%    -7 bps \par  2-10-Year Swap Curve  12 bps   0 bp \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar added to recent gains in Asian trade Friday on broad strength in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and traders say the unit could remain near the strong end of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 "The (U.S. dollar) is looking heavy against the rest of Asia...the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may come in to slow the Singapore dollar's strength, but it can remain strong," said a dealer at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore advocates a modest and gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said the next support level for the U.S. dollar is near S$1.5630, but the dollar has potential to move lower.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer also said that the Singapore dollar is a "very close proxy" for the Chinese yuan, which Friday reached a new high against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At 0730 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at CNY7.8891. Earlier in the session, it dipped to CNY7.8870, the lowest since Beijing moved to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in July 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Market players may buy the relatively liquid Singapore dollar in lieu of a long Chinese yuan position, as the two units tend to move in tandem.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore bond market, prices followed U.S. Treasurys higher in early action, but retraced their gains as the overnight rate moved higher.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight rate reached 3.63% in late trade Friday, bringing the cost of short-term funding above bond yields across maturities.  \par \par \uc2 A trader at a local bank said that despite a five-basis-point drop in U.S. Treasury yields overnight, Singapore government paper will remain under pressure due to relatively high short-term funding costs.  \par \par \uc2 "There is negative funding throughout the curve...it will be a major challenge for bonds," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 "We had buying interest in the morning, when yields were originally down three to four basis points in the long end...now they're down maybe one basis point," the trader added.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 27-10-06 0844GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061027e2ar000mu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
