\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Growth To Moderate But Stay Healthy In 07 -MAS  \b0\par\par\uc2 736 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2006\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's growth will likely slow to a moderate pace in 2007, as high oil prices and weaker external demand weigh on the domestic economy, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "For 2006 as a whole, Singapore's GDP growth is expected to come in at the upper half of the official forecast range of 6.5% to 7.5%. The economy is likely to register more moderate growth in 2007 in line with its medium-term potential rate," the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said in its semiannual macroeconomic review.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore's medium-term potential growth rate is estimated at 3% to 5%.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said that high energy price will contribute to a lower rate of economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 "Demand-supply conditions suggest that global oil prices could stay high, at above $60 per barrel," it said.  \par \par \uc2 After reaching a high of around $78 dollars in July, Nymex crude has pulled back recently to levels just under $60 a barrel.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said that while a sharp global downturn is unlikely, it does anticipate slower expansion worldwide.  \par \par \uc2 "Both developed countries and emerging Asian economies are predicted to record slower growth in 2007 compared to this year," it said.  \par \par \uc2 "Nevertheless, the outlook remains generally sanguine and should support continued healthy expansion."  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said Singapore's mainstay electronics sector will experience "knock-on" effects from lower demand, but the cycle is not in a typical downturn.  \par \par \uc2 "On balance, barring a sharp adjustment in the U.S. economy, the global IT industry will grow at a slightly slower pace in 2007 compared to this year."  \par \par \uc2 The report cites industry analyst projections of 6% to 8% growth in chip sales in 2007 compared with 8% to 10% growth in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Biomedical manufacturing, which is another important sector of the economy, may continue growing at a steady clip, as pharmaceutical plants add capacity in 2007, the bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS also highlighted the construction sector, which it expects to benefit from developments in the Marina Bay area and a recent increase in residential housing prices.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation In Check, Policy On Track  \par \par \uc2 Despite some passthrough effects of higher energy costs in 2006, consumer prices have been contained by relatively low import price inflation and subdued pressure on wages.  \par \par \uc2 Nominal wage growth is expected to be just 3.5% to 4.0% in both 2006 and 2007, comparable to 3.5% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The recent strengthening of the Singapore dollar has helped shield Singapore against external price pressure, the main source of inflation in the city-state.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS advocates a modest and gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS reaffirmed its slightly contractionary monetary policy on October 10, and it expects the current policy will serve to contain inflation through next year.  \par \par \uc2 "CPI inflation will be contained in 2006 and 2007 under the current policy stance," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said that the Singapore dollar has stayed in the upper half of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy band since April.  \par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of 2006, the Singapore dollar has appreciated by about 5% against the U.S. dollar, which began the year around S$1.6620 and is now near S$1.5770.  \par \par \uc2 However, the MAS said the real exchange rate has remained low due to low costs and prices in Singapore relative to its trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 "The level of Singapore's real exchange rate at this point of the business cycle suggests that our competitiveness has not been undermined by the appreciation of the trade-weighted (Singapore dollar)," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 Monetary conditions tightened earlier this year, as the Singapore dollar three-month interbank rate rose to 3.56% in August from 3.44% in May.  \par \par \uc2 The three-month interbank rate retraced to 3.44% at the end of the third quarter, and the MAS expects it to hold near the current level through the fourth quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar three-month interbank rate is "expected to stabilize at around current levels for the rest of 2006, against the backdrop of a pause in US Fed funds rate hikes in August and September," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Stephen Wright [ 25-10-06 0400GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061025e2ap0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
