\par\par \b \uc2 DATA VIEW: Singapore Inflation At 14-Month Low In Sept  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Jannarone  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 2006\par 08:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's inflation rate remained below the government's official forecast range in September, and economists say that stable price levels give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  room to ease policy in the event of a sharp economic slowdown.  \par \par \uc2 The consumer price index rose 0.4% from a year earlier in September, the slowest pace since July 2005, according to official figures released Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has a target range for inflation of 1% to 2% for 2006, and it expects similarly low inflation of 0.5% to 1.5% in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 Seasonally adjusted, the consumer price index contracted 0.1% from August after remaining unchanged the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 September's data were below the average of a Dow Jones Newswires poll of seven economists, which forecast the CPI would rise by 0.7% from a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank economist Alvin Liew said he expects the current monetary policy to keep prices well-contained through 2006.  \par \par \uc2 "The strong Singapore dollar will be quite effective in limiting import price inflation in the last months of the year," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He added that ahead of Monday's CPI release, UOB had forecast full-year inflation between 1.3% and 1.4%, but that it now forecasts inflation between 1.1% and 1.2% for 2006.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore advocates a modest and gradual appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of 2006, the Singapore dollar has appreciated by about 6% against the U.S. dollar, which began the year around S$1.6620 and is now at S$1.5742.  \par \par \uc2 The stronger Singapore dollar has helped shield Singapore against external price pressure, the main source of inflation in the city-state.  \par \par \uc2 Action Economics economist Mayank Mehta said lower oil costs also benefited consumers in September, when prices in the transport and communication sector eased.  \par \par \uc2 Prices in the sector, which accounts for 22% of the inflation index, fell 2.7% from a year earlier in September.  \par \par \uc2 "We haven't seen the full impact from lower oil prices as yet so we can expect it later this year," Mehta said. "We may revise our inflation forecast down."  \par \par \uc2 Action Economics forecasts 1.2% full-year inflation for 2006, but may lower its prediction to 1.1%, Mehta said.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS recently noted in a report that "domestic petrol companies...cut pump prices twice by a total of 11 cents in September following the plunge in global oil prices."  \par \par \uc2 After reaching a high of around $78 dollars in July, Nymex crude is now trading below $60 per barrel.  \par \par \uc2 Liew said the risk of inflation won't prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from loosening monetary conditions if global economic prospects deteriorate.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the benign situation for inflation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may switch to a neutral policy...but it would only need to change its stance in the unexpected event of a drastic change in the external environment," Liew said.  \par \par \uc2 CIMB-GK economist Song Seng Wun said monetary policy is "on track" for now, but that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could switch its stance in April, when it will hold its next policy review, if the economy falters or overheats.  \par \par \uc2 "Until we see signs of much stronger growth or much slower growth, this inflation level would be supportive of a reiteration of a monetary policy that is keeping inflationary pressure at bay," Song said.  \par \par \uc2 Among other key components, housing costs, which make up 21% of the index, gained 2.0% from a year earlier, and fell 0.5% from August due to lower maintenance charges and garbage collection fees.  \par \par \uc2 The cost of food, which is the biggest component in the index with a 23% weighting, climbed 1.8% from a year earlier and held steady from August.  \par \par \uc2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 23-10-06 0650GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020061023e2an000fu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
