\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Sing dlr, Thai baht at multi-week highs  \b0\par\par\uc2 846 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2006\par 04:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 20 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged up on Friday, with the Singapore dollar, Thai baht and Taiwan dollar hitting multi-week highs as weak U.S. data hurt the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A survey by the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia on Thursday showed a surprise fall in business activity in the U.S. Mid-Atlantic region, renewing worries about U.S. economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose to a six-week high at 1.5710 per U.S. dollar <SGD=>, making traders wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht gained a quarter of a percent to a one-month peak at about 37.20 per dollar <THB=>, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> rose as much as half a percent to 33.02 to the U.S. dollar -- its strongest level in about 2-\u189\'00\'BD weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen was up about a third of a percent at 118.30 per dollar <JPY=>, while the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was steady near Thursday's one-month peaks. The Philippine peso held around 50 per dollar <PHP=>.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meanwhile, set the mid-point of the yuan's exchange rate against the dollar <CNY=CFXS> at 7.8995, its highest mid-point since the July 2005 revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 "After a rough night for the dollar, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are keeping in step with the move in dollar/yen," said Sean Callow, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Bank in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 A rally in local share markets also provided support.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea's key stock index <.KS11> closed up 0.75 percent, Taiwan's share index <.TWII> rose 0.6 percent and Singapore's benchmark Straits Times Index <.STI> pushed up to a record high as overnight gains on Wall Street boosted regional markets.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said firm equity markets suggested investor risk appetite remained high despite tensions over North Korea.  \par \par \uc2 They added that despite weakness in overnight U.S. data, there was still uncertainty about the U.S. economic outlook and this kept most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in familiar ranges versus the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "When it comes to the dollar, there still doesn't seem to be a strong conviction on where it is headed," said Philip Wee, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS Bank. "I'm looking ahead to the Fed next week and that could provide some direction."  \par \par \uc2 SING DOLLAR SHINES  \par \par \uc2 In Singapore, this week's 1 percent gain in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the U.S. dollar made some traders nervous about \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- although the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) has not been spotted in the market this week.  \par \par \uc2 There have been differing views on whether Singapore dollar gains are related to expectations of Singapore state investment firm Temasek [TEM.UL] selling shares in Thai telecommunications group Shin Corp. to resolve a foreign-ownership dispute.  \par \par \uc2 The Shin Corp. sale by the family of former Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra sparked street protests in Thailand aimed at forcing him to quit before he was ousted in September's military coup. Critics have said the Shin deal broke foreign ownership rules.  \par \par \uc2 "There are no flows related to Temasek as far as I can see," said a Singapore trader. "I'm not aware of the MAS being in the market today," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Wee at DBS said the Singapore dollar probably remained at the strong end of its undisclosed trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band. He estimated the mid-point of the band at about 1.5870 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A trader in Bangkok said strong offshore demand for the baht was helping to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/baht is lower because of some capital inflows from offshore," he said. "I think it's related to direct investment."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0738 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.34         118.65       +0.26\par  Sing dlr        1.5708         1.5736       +0.18\par  Taiwan dlr      33.190         33.199       +0.03\par  Korean won      957.20         957.40       +0.02\par  Baht             37.26          37.33       +0.19\par  Peso             50.11          50.02       -0.19\par  Rupiah         9155.00        9153.00       -0.02\par  Rupee            45.33          45.31       -0.04\par  Ringgit         3.6730         3.6690       -0.11\par  Yuan            7.9032         7.9096       +0.08\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.34         117.96       -0.32\par  Sing dlr        1.5708         1.6625       +5.84\par  Taiwan dlr      33.190         32.850       -1.02\par  Korean won      957.20        1011.60       +5.68\par  Baht             37.26          41.11      +10.33\par  Peso             50.11          53.09       +5.95\par  Rupiah         9155.00        9835.00       +7.43\par  Rupee            45.33          45.04       -0.64\par  Ringgit         