\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up On US Bank USD Selling; Bonds Flat  \b0\par\par\uc2 361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2006\par 10:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest   Change\par USD/SGD               1.5723   -0.0030\par Overnight Rate        3.19%    -19 bps\par 2 Year Bond Yield     3.02%       -\par 10-Year Bond Yield    3.21%       -\par 2-Year Swap Offer     3.48%     -3 bps\par 10-Year Swap Offer    3.63%     -1 bps\par 2-10-Year Swap Curve  15 bps    +2 bps\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was slightly higher in Asian trade Friday with losses limited by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. The U.S. dollar is "likely to be stuck at S$1.5700 next week," a foreign bank dealer said. "(But if) the dollar drops below Y118...the Singapore dollar is likely going to appreciate." At 0620 GMT, the greenback was trading at Y118.32. However, traders said there was market talk that options players are supporting the U.S. dollar at Y118 in the short term. But some market participants believe the Singapore dollar will strengthen next week. "The current sentiment is to sell the dollar against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," another foreign bank dealer said. "Moreover, the Singapore economy looks good, and oil prices are lower. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar should rise unless the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Traders suspected MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, as local banks were seen buying the USD/SGD pair at S$1.5720 after U.S. banks sold the pair on the topside. The MAS' key monetary policy tool is managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed trading band. In the local debt market, Singapore government bonds were flat. "There was no trade except for the longer-dated bonds," said Selena Ling, head of treasury research at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp. "Everyone's on holiday." She said trade is likely to be quiet at least until the results of the U.S. Federal Open Committee Meeting are known late in the global day Wednesday. Many regional markets, including Singapore, Malaysia and Indonesia, are closed for one or more days next week for the end of the Muslim holy month of Ramadan. -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires, 65-6415-4118, weiyi.lim@dowjones.com -Edited by Michael Kitchen -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020061020e2ak003pd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
