\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yen, yuan behind rally, c.banks seen  \b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2006\par 05:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 5 (Reuters) - A rising Japanese yen and Chinese yuan pushed Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to multi-week highs on Tuesday, prompting the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  verbally.  \par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> rallied to 2-week highs near 115.60 per dollar after Monday's capital spending reinforced expectations for rate rises in Japan. Those expectations had been subdued by soft July inflation data.  \par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> hit its highest level since being freed from a virtual dollar peg last July. The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=>, buoyed by official comments made last week that a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would help contain inflation, rose to 3.6445 per dollar, its highest in two months.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> rallied less than a quarter of a percent, although it hit 50.33 per dollar, its strongest in four years. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Amando Tetangco said the strong peso posed a risk to inflation because of the additional supply of funds.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose to 1.5658 per U.S. dollar, its highest since mid-May. Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, ensuring it remained within its secret, trade-weighted policy band.  \par \par \uc2 "The MAS seems quite determined not to let the NEER (nominal effective exchange rate) move beyond the band. It is still sitting close to the upper limit," said Sabrina Jacobs, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> barely budged after Bank Indonesia cut its one-month policy rate by 50 basis points, as many in the market had expected.  \par \par \uc2 YEN POSITIONS  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rally was spurred by heavy squaring of short yen positions. Data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission showed bets against the yen hit a record high in the week to Aug. 29.  \par \par \uc2 Sean Callow, Westpac Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said the yen's rally was driven more by "fear and greed" and short covering rather than fundamentals, such as the strong capital spending and rising Nikkei average <.N225>.  \par \par \uc2 Some of the Japanese data preceding this week's capital spending numbers had been weaker than expected, particularly the rise in the July consumer price index (CPI) that tempered market expectations of monetary tightening.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/yen historically goes up by the stairs, down in the elevator. But unless the G7/IMF is huge, then this is not the big move," Callow said.  \par \par \uc2 Callow was referring to the World Bank and IMF annual meetings taking place in Singapore later this month.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0822 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.60         116.19       +0.51\par  Sing dlr        1.5659         1.5685       +0.17\par  Taiwan dlr      32.768         32.740       -0.09\par  Korean won      956.50         957.70       +0.13\par  Baht             37.34          37.34       +0.01\par  Peso             50.37          50.46       +0.18\par  Rupiah         9078.00        9080.00       +0.02\par  Rupee   