\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Firmer thanks to yen, yuan, stocks  \b0\par\par\uc2 903 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2006\par 04:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 4 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were broadly firm on Monday as the Japanese yen rebounded from last week's lows, the Chinese yuan hit a fresh post-revaluation high and regional equity markets posted strong gains.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's data showing the U.S. economy created 128,000 jobs in August, just above expectations, did little to alter the view that U.S. interest rates would be on hold for the rest of the year, adding momentum to the Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. numbers were not particularly good and there continues to be a scaling back of rate-hike expectations," said BNP Paribas \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Thio Chin Loo.  \par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> rose about three quarters of a percent from levels traded late on Friday, lifted by news on Monday that Japanese firms increased capital spending by 16.6 percent in the second quarter compared with a year earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> broke through a key chart level to hit its highest in about 3-\u189\'00\'BD months at 1.5665 per U.S. dollar, while the Philippine peso <PHP=> extended last week's gains to rise to a four-year high at 50.48 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> and Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> added roughly a third of a percent each. The Thai baht <THB=> rose about 0.5 percent to a 2-1/2-week high at about 37.33 per dollar, while the Malaysian ringgit broke out of recent ranges to firm to 3.6550 per dollar, its highest in almost a month.  \par \par \uc2 The yuan <CNY=CFXS> strengthened as far as 7.9400 per dollar, its highest since it was revalued in July 2005.  \par \par \uc2 It was expected to remain in focus ahead of this month's meeting of finance ministers of the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised nations, with China facing pressure to step up the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit are doing well because there is a risk of concerted pressure on the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Richard Yetsenga, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in Hong Kong. "They are the ones to benefit."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SUSPECTED  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said they suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to cap gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which they felt was probably trading at the strong end of its secret trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2 Others said it was less clear.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not too sure about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- but one of the local banks has been selling Sing dollars and that is surprising," said a trader. "With 2-3 weeks left to the next MAS policy review, everyone is trying to second guess what they will do."  \par \par \uc2 The latest Reuters poll on market positioning in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests investors have stepped up long Singapore dollar positions versus the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Taking a long position on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is essentially a bet that it will strengthen.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the ringgit continued to benefit from positive sentiment following Friday's 2007 Malaysian budget.  \par \par \uc2 The budget unveiled the first cut in corporate tax since 1998 and dealers said this, together with a comment in the economic report also released on Friday that a stronger ringgit would help contain inflation, boosted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "About 80 percent of the move in the ringgit is related to the budget reaction," said a Kuala Lumpur-based trader.  \par \par \uc2 "There were comments from finance ministry officials about the ringgit being used to fight inflation and this saw an immediate 100-point reaction which is continuing today."  \par \par \uc2 Malaysia's benchmark stock index <.KLSE> rose to its highest level in almost four months, bolstering the ringgit.  \par \par \uc2 Other Asian equity markets were also firmer and in Taiwan foreign funds bought a net 10.71 billion Taiwan dollars of shares compared with about 6.8 billion on Friday.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0808 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.19         117.31       +0.96\par  Sing dlr        1.5685         1.5715       +0.19\par  Taiwan dlr      32.740         32.884       +0.44\par  Korean won      957.70         960.50       +0.29\par  Baht             37.34          37.51       +0.46\par  Peso             50.46          50.74       +0.54\par  Rupiah         9080.00        9093.00       +0.14\par  Rupee            46.31          46.53       +0.46\par  Ringgit         3.6565         3.6710       +0.40\par  Yuan            7.9401         7.9543       +0.18\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.19         117.96       +1.52\par  Sing dlr        1.5685         1.6625       +5.99\par  Taiwan dlr      32.740         32.850       +0.34\par  Korean won      957.