\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Tracks Yen Strength; MAS Seen Paring Gains  \b0\par\par\uc2 595 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2006\par 10:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose through key support levels Monday, tracking strength in the Japanese yen, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to contain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 "The Japanese yen started the day stronger, and the capital expenditures numbers from Japan gave a lift to both the yen and the Singapore dollar," said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar followed the yen higher in early trade, gaining against the U.S. dollar, but players say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market to cut its appreciation short.  \par \par \uc2 "(The U.S. dollar) fell to S$1.5660 with the rising yen, and there is talk the MAS is around to support it at S$1.5670," a trader at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore manages the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an unspecified band, and traders say the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the U.S. dollar was slightly stronger, above the S$1.57 level.  \par \par \uc2 But a strategist at a foreign bank said the value of the Singapore dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not changed from last week, even though the U.S. dollar declined to S$1.5670 in intraday trade Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket, including the Japanese yen and the Malaysian ringgit, have gained in recent days, reducing the value of the Singapore dollar against the overall basket.  \par \par \uc2 At 0811 GMT, the U.S. dollar traded at Y116.20, down Y0.90 from Friday's New York close.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.5698, from S$1.5721 at the close of New York trade Friday and S$1.5722 in late Asian trade Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the U.S. dollar to be supported at S$1.5670 and face resistance at S$1.5720 in the next few days.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore government debt market, prices fell slightly across maturities, and the yield curve flattened modestly.  \par \par \uc2 Despite a modest bid for short-dated U.S. Treasurys in Friday's global session, Singapore players shunned short-dated Singapore government paper after poor results at an auction for Singapore three-month bills.  \par \par \uc2 The cutoff yield was 3.23% for the S$1.6 billion auction of bills.  \par \par \uc2 "The auction results that were weaker than expected had a negative effect on the two-year note, and its yield was up two or three basis points," said Selena Ling, strategist at OCBC Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Activity was light in the long end beyond the seven-year note," Ling said.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year Singapore government bond bid-side yield was two basis points higher at 3.14%, while the 10-year bid-side yield rose one basis point to 3.38%.  \par \par \uc2 There will be little economic data from the U.S. this week, but the market will watch for an indication of the Bank of Japan's future policy when the Bank holds a meeting Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest  Previous \par Overnight rate        3.06%   3.13% \par Two-year bond yield:  3.14%   3.12% \par 10-year bond yield:   3.38%   3.37% \par Two-year swap offer:  3.58%   3.56% \par 10-year swap offer:   3.67%   3.67% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:   9bps   11bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 04-09-06 0849GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060904e294000eu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
