\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Hits  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Policy Ceiling  \b0\par\par\uc2 653 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2006\par 10:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar edged higher in Asian trade Friday but encountered resistance as it reached the strong end of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's undisclosed trading band.  \par \par \uc2 'Everyone wants to be short the (U.S.) dollar against Asia, but the Singapore dollar basket is trading at the high end of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range,' a dealer at a foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore manages the value of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a basket of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within an unspecified band, and market players say the MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this week to stem gains in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar. \par \par   'MAS came in to bring (the U.S. dollar) back up to the S$1.5715-S$1.5720 level,' the dealer said. \par \par   At 0636 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.5722, from S$1.5732 at the close of New York trade and S$1.5717 in late Asian trade Thursday. \par \par   Some players use the relatively liquid Singapore dollar to take on exposure to Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which many believe have the potential to gain against the U.S. dollar in the coming months. \par \par   'Only Asia has been lacking against the U.S. dollar. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from other regions - like the euro, British pound and Australian dollar - have already gained quite a bit,' said Emmanuel Ng, a strategist at OCBC bank. \par \par   'The big picture is that the market expects some Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation, and the Singapore dollar also reflects strength in (the Chinese yuan),' Ng said. \par   But some analysts think the Singapore dollar has already traded outside of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target band for the last few sessions, and some players may take short positions as they perceive upside risk to be minimal. \par   'Gains are seen as limited for the Singapore dollar, so it is a good funding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for a carry trade with the Indonesian rupiah or Philippine peso, which have higher interest rates,' a local dealer said. \par   Investors can benefit from interest rate differentials by taking loans in Singapore dollars and buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that offer higher rates of interest on deposits. \par    Traders expect the U.S. dollar to be supported at S$1.5680 and face resistance at S$1.5750 next week. \par \par   In the Singapore government debt market, longer-dated issues posted significant gains after an overnight rally brought yields on U.S. 10-year Treasury notes to a five-month low. \par   'The three or four basis-point fall in yields was a catch-up from earlier this week,' a local trader said. \par   On Wednesday and Thursday, Singapore government bonds held steady, despite gains in U.S. Treasurys. \par \par   The two-year Singapore government bond bid-side yield was one basis point lower at 3.13%, while the 10- and 15-year bid-side yields each fell four basis points to 3.36% and 3.43%, respectively. \par   'The overnight rate is still high, so the market was buying the long end of the curve to avoid negative carry,' another trader said. \par \par   Recently, short-dated bond yields have fallen beneath the overnight rate. \par \par   Traders say the timing of a possible interest rate cut in the U.S. is the bond market's key focus in coming weeks. \par \par   The release of the U.S. August nonfarm payroll figure, slated for tonight, may give an indication of whether the U.S. economy is cooling, and how soon the Fed may cut rates. \par \par   'Payrolls will be the main focus into next week,' a local trader said. \par                       Latest  Previous \par Overnight rate        3.13%   3.25% \par Two-year bond yield:  3.13%   3.14% \par 10-year bond yield:   3.36%   3.40% \par Two-year swap offer:  3.56%   3.57% \par 10-year swap offer:   3.67%   3.69% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  11bps   12bps \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4152; john.jannarone@dowjones.com -Edited by Graham Norris -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060901e291003ju\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
