\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Up Late On PM's Speech; Bonds Weaker  \b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2006\par 10:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose in Asian trade Monday in response to the prime minister's optimistic speech about the economy Sunday and a stronger euro. "The Singapore dollar is quite strong...so there's concern of (Monetary of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in the market," said Jimmy Koh, a treasurys economist from United Overseas Bank. "Traders may not want to push the Singapore dollar significantly higher."  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore manages the value of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an unspecified policy band.  \par \par \uc2 At 0746 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.5706, down from S$1.5737 at the New York close and from S$1.5740 late Friday in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 A foreign bank trader said that investors would be looking out for Japan's July consumer price index released this Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "A strong Japanese CPI will result in a stronger yen, which may lead to a more bearish U.S. dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He also said investors would be paying attention to any change in China's policy on the yuan after last Friday's Chinese interest rate increase sparked more speculation that the government may let the yuan appreciate at a faster pace.  \par \par \uc2 China's benchmark one-year bank lending rates and one-year deposit rates rose by 0.27 percentage point to 6.12% and 2.52% from last Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar also got a boost from a speech by Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong, the foreign bank trader said. "Foreign investors feel positive about his speech."  \par \par \uc2 Lee said Singapore's economy is expected to grow about 7.0% this year. Over the long term, he expects gross domestic product growth to average 3%-5% a year. He also welcomed more foreigners to work in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar also benefited from the undermining of sentiment in the U.S. dollar resulting from a strengthening euro, the trader said. At 0742 GMT, the euro was trading at US$1.2894, up from US$1.2839 at the New York close.  \par \par \uc2 Traders predict the greenback to be supported slightly below S$1.5700, with resistance at S$1.5740-50.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore bond market, the two-year bond yield rose four basis points to 3.07%, while the 10-year bond yield rose two basis points to 3.40%.  \par \par \uc2 "There's more selling in the short end because the three-month (Singapore treasury bill) was not so well-received," a local dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 The three-month Singapore treasury bill was sold in an auction at 3.15% today, compared with 3.1% last week.  \par \par \uc2 The government will auction 15-year bonds Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest  Previous \par  Overnight rate        3.19%   3.19% \par  Two-year bond yield:  3.07%   3.03% \par  10-year bond yield:   3.40%   3.38% \par  Two-year swap offer:  3.54%   3.57% \par  10-year swap offer:   3.72%   3.75% \par  2-10-yr swap spread:  18 bps  18 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Wei Yi Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4118; weiyi.lim@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Graham Norris [ 21-08-06 0812GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI00