\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down On Rumored MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ;Bonds Down  \b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2006\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell in Asian trade Friday, which traders suspect was due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its trading band.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the U.S. dollar is likely to continue to gain in the near term, as the MAS is still monitoring the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other factors, such as key data and world events, could add to the greenback's strength.  \par \par \uc2 "The important technical level would be S$1.5770-S$1.5780," said a dealer from a foreign bank. "If it breaks above this level, we can see some short-covering of the U.S. dollar."  \par \par \uc2 At 0644 GMT, the U.S. unit was trading at $1.5756, up from S$1.5714 at the New York close and $1.5687 late Thursday in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank treasury economist Jimmy Koh said investors should be alert to the possibility of China announcing more flexibility for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the next few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 China is likely to face more pressure from the international community at the International Monetary Fund meeting in September and may adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy ahead of the meeting so it's not "seen as bowing to international pressure," he said.  \par \par \uc2 If this happens, the U.S. dollar will have a downside bias, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The foreign bank dealer said the Singapore dollar was trading 2% above the suspected band early Friday on selling of the greenback by speculative sellers. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar soon turned around, which prompted rumors of MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a band but doesn't disclose its parameters, leaving market participants to estimate the lower and upper limits of the trading band.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak yen did not help the Singapore dollar either. Japanese oil companies were buying a lot of the U.S. dollar against the yen," the foreign bank dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 At GMT 0646, the U.S. dollar was trading at Y115.69, up from Y115.27 at New York's close.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore bond market, the two-year yield rose three basis points to 2.95%, while the 10-year yield rose three basis points to 3.40%.  \par \par \uc2 "A local bank started selling the 15-year bonds and it started a downturn in the bond market," a dealer from a local bank said.  \par \par \uc2 He said traders will be eyeing the tender of the 15-year Singapore Government Securities late next week.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                        Latest  Previous \par  Overnight rate        3.38%   3.25% \par  Two-year bond yield:  2.95%   2.92% \par  10-year bond yield:   3.40%   3.37% \par  Two-year swap offer:  3.57%   3.60% \par  10-year swap offer:   3.76%   3.80% \par  2-10-yr swap spread:  19 bps  20 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By 