\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Higher as Fed rate pause anticipated  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2006\par 04:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose across the board on Monday, as weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data on Friday lifted expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve would this week pause a two-year campaign of raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2  Upbeat news on the Philippine economy at the weekend helped boost the peso <PHP=> to a three-month high at 51.33 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose to 1.5704 to the U.S. dollar, its highest in about 2-\u189\'00\'BD   months.  \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=> set its strongest level in almost three months at 37.68 a dollar and the Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> added 0.5 percent to 3.6550 per per dollar.  \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won <KRW=>, which took a beating last week on worries about the country's deteriorating trade balance, rose to levels near 961 per dollar, up 0.7 percent from Thursday's four-month lows around 967.  \par \par \uc2  The dollar held near two-month lows hit against the euro <EUR=> and yen <JPY=> on Friday after data showed the U.S. economy created 113,000 new jobs in July.  \par \par \uc2  This tilted market expectations towards the U.S. Fed leaving its key interest rate at 5.25 percent when it meets on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  "The sell off in the dollar is coming on the back of slower growth in the U.S. and that environment is not particularly positive for Asian growth and equity markets," said Craig Chan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2  "But in a soft-dollar backdrop, the dollar is probably the predominant theme. If you look at correlations, the correlation between Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dollar/yen is much stronger than with equity markets."  \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said high oil prices, which surged more than 2 percent on Monday, and a U.S. economic slowdown that could hurt equity markets were reasons to be cautious about chasing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too high for now.  \par \par \uc2  "The best bets in the region for a short-term rally are the rupee, the ringgit, and Taiwan dollar as these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have already priced in negative local news to the greatest extent," ABN AMRO senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Shahab Jalinoos said in a note.  \par \par \uc2  "The won still looks the most vulnerable regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as it is only just starting the process of pricing in negative local developments."  \par \par \uc2  PESO EXTENDS GAINS  \par \par \uc2  A rally in the Philippine peso gathered pace after news at the weekend that government revenues from a broadened sales tax in the first six months were 23 percent above target and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had raised its 2006 growth projection on remittances from overseas Filipino workers.  \par \par \uc2  "There is a bunch of good news moving the peso," said a Manila trader. "This is the reason why we have already breached the 51.40 level, an important technical level since last Friday."  \par \par \uc2  Traders in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they were wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) to curb gains in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although there have been few signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2  "Probably they are seeing some pass through of imported inflation on the economy and so are essentially happier with a stronger Sing in the extreme short term," said a trader.  \par \par \uc2  Lim Hng Kiang, Singapore's minister for trade and industry and deputy chairman for the MAS, said on Monday the country's exports are growing despite the strengthening of the Singapore dollar.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0751 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.65         115.26       +0.53\par  Sing dlr        1.5704         1.5765       +0.39\par  Taiwan dlr      32.809         32.864       +0.17\par  Korean won      962.40         964.80       +0.25\par  Baht             37.72          37.85       +0.34\par  Peso             51.44          51.52       +0.16\par  Rupiah         9070.00        9090.00       +0.22\par  Rupee            46.50          46.55       +0.11\par  Ringgit         3.6600         3.6735       +0.37\par  Yuan            7.9695         7.9787       +0.12\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.65         117.96       +2.89\par  Sing dlr        1.5704         1.6625       +5.86\par  Taiwan dlr      32.809         32.850       +0.12\par  Korean won      962.40        1011.60       +5.11\par  Baht             37.72          41.11       +8.99\par  Peso             51.44          53.09       +3.21\par  Rupiah         9070.00        9835.00       +8.43\par  Rupee            46.50          45.04       -3.13\par  Ringgit         3.6600         3.7790       +3.25\par  Yuan            7.9695         8.0702       +1.26\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|A