\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Up after Fed survey suggests US slowdown  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2006\par 04:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, July 27 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed on Thursday after a survey by the Federal Reserve that suggested the U.S. economy may be slowing, which renewed talk that U.S. interest rates could soon peak after two years of rising.  \par \par \uc2  The Fed's Beige Book survey, released on Wednesday, said U.S. economic activity grew overall between June and mid-July, but that there were many local reports of slowing growth.  \par \par \uc2  That set the dollar up for sharp losses against the yen and euro, setting the tone for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trade, with many regionals hiting two-week highs.  \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> rose to 9,045 per dollar, its strongest since July 12, but it had given up most of its gains by late trade.  \par \par \uc2  A dealer in Jakarta said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  orders to buy dollars around 9,060-9,070 had capped rupiah gains, while some investors stepped in around that area to take profits.  \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want to see the rupiah strengthen too quickly," he said.  \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> rose for the third straight day to 51.51 per dollar, its strongest level in about 2-\u189\'00\'BD   months. It has gained about 1.25 percent so far this week.  \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won <KRW=> and Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> gained about a third of a percent each.  \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht <THB=> hit a two-week peak at about 37.78 per dollar, while the Malaysian ringgit firmed to 3.6685, also the highest in about two weeks.  \par \par \uc2  TIME FOR RISK?  \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rally in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested investors were slowly moving back into riskier assets after a rout in emerging markets in May and early June.  \par \par \uc2  "While the Beige Book has reinforced the move in emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it had already started well ahead of that," said Sabrina Jacobs, emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for Asia at Dresdner Kleinwort.  \par \par \uc2  "The correlation between nominal yields and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance has been very high and positive since around mid-June, a time when risk aversion started to moderate."  \par \par \uc2  Jacobs said the move became obvious across markets on Wednesday, with fresh demand for emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the South African rand.  \par \par \uc2  In Asia, high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the rupiah and peso are expected to benefit the most from a pick-up in risk appetite.  \par \par \uc2  Rupiah sentiment was boosted on Wednesday by Standard & Poor's upgrading of Indonesia's credit rating.  \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> firmed to its highest in more than two weeks at 1.5775 to the U.S. dollar and analysts said it remained at the strong end of its secret, trade-weighted band.  \par \par \uc2  Data on Wednesday showing Singapore's factory output grew at the fastest pace in more than two years in June bolstered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2  One trader said there had been some talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap its strength but it was not clear the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.  \par \par \uc2  "I have been expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 6-7 days now and we continue to trade at the top end of the band," another trader said. "The MAS is probably looking at a flow we in the market don't know about. Once that flow is through, they may come in and buy U.S. dollars."  \par \par \uc2  The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS>, meanwhile, rose to 7.9760 per U.S. dollar, the highest level since its July 2005 revaluation, supporting other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Change on the day at   0809 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.89         116.73       +0.72\par  Sing dlr        1.5778         1.5832       +0.34\par  Taiwan dlr      32.754         32.849       +0.29\par  Korean won      952.00         954.60       +0.27\par  Baht             37.80          37.96       +0.42\par  Peso             51.52          51.74       +0.43\par  Rupiah         9068.00        9073.00       +0.06\par  Rupee            46.63          46.80       +0.38\par  Ringgit         3.6710         3.6800       +0.25\par  Yuan            7.9757         7.9852       +0.12\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       115.89         117.96       +1.79\par  Sing dlr        1.5778         1.6625       +5.37\par  Taiwan dlr      32.754         32.850       +0.29\par  Korean won      952.00        1011.60       +6.26\par  Baht             37.80          41.11       +8.76\par  Peso             51.52          53.09       +3.05\par  Rupiah         9068.00        9835.00       +8.46\par  Rupee            46.63          45.04       -3.40\par  Ringgit         3.