\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Stall near highs, Korean  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  seen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2006\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , April 25 (Reuters) - Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks in South Korea and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and a pause in the Japanese yen's rally led to a slight retreat in the won, Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The won, Sing dollar, Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah had hit fresh multi-year highs in early trade after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet called for an adjustment in Asian exchange rates, echoing statements from the Group of Seven nations and the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> climbed to a fresh 8-1/2-year high near 936 per dollar overnight and was underpinned by data showing faster-than-expected first-quarter economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 It slipped later and shed more than half a percent after the Bank of Korea's suspected dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "With the big move in dollar/yen over, the fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bubbled to the surface again and we may see a short squeeze in Asia," said Ben Simpfendorfer, a regional economist with The Royal Bank of Scotland in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 Although loss of momentum in the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had stalled the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP>, the support from continuing equity inflows would eventually push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 31.50 per U.S. dollar, Simpfendorfer said.  \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar rose to 31.86 per U.S. dollar, its highest in more than 3 months, but then pulled back to 31.97/99.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Asian rally was also undermined by the yuan's failure to strengthen despite renewed pressure from Western policy-makers for China to play a greater role in the adjustment of global trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the central point of the yuan's trading band for Tuesday at 8.0160 per dollar, weaker than Monday's close at 8.0150. It later rose marginally to 8.0120.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> retained some gains but retreated to levels around 1.5880/85 per U.S. dollar from the overnight high of 1.5850, its strongest level in nearly 8 years.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht <THB=> was briefly quoted at a 6-year high of 37.48 per dollar, before it followed the regional trend.  \par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> pulled back to levels around 8,800 from around 8,780 per dollar, its strongest level since May 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> remained an exception to the wider trend in Asia, undermined by concerns over the country's fiscal position. It continued falling and hit a 2-month low of 51.90 per dollar. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0800 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.42         115.07       +0.57\par  Sing dlr        1.5882         1.5901       +0.12\par  Taiwan dlr      31.982         31.982       +0.00\par  Korean won      945.00         939.60       -0.57\par  Baht             37.60          37.57       -0.08\par  Peso             51.87          51.64       -0.44\par  Rupiah         8800.00        8820.00       +0.23\par  Rupee            44.90          44.98       +0.18\par  Ringgit        