\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Slightly Up; Bonds Gain On Local Buying  \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2006\par 11:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose slightly in Asia Friday, tracking early weakness in the U.S. dollar, but more gains were capped by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices rallied, led by local buying in the 10-year paper, which unexpectedly flattened the yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    At 0659 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.5980, compared with S$1.5985 at the close of New York trade. In late Asian trade Thursday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.5981. \par   The U.S. dollar traded in a range of S$1.5974 and S$1.6002. \par   Dealers said trading was lackluster as the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tracked the U.S. dollar's movement against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par   After slipping to Y117.38 early, the U.S. dollar stabilized to Y117.61 in afternoon trade, little changed from the New York close. \par   Comments by Singapore's trade minister about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also failed to move the market. \par   'The adjustment in the Singapore dollar is due to market forces, and it also reflects the strength of the Singapore economy,' Trade and Industry Minister Lim Hng Kiang told reporters Friday. \par   The Singapore dollar is near an eight-year high and has risen 6.6% since late November when the rally began. \par   Recent suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been preventing a decline in the U.S. dollar after it recovered from Thursday's intraday low of S$1.5959 - the lowest in eight years. \par   BNP Paribas said in a note the U.S. dollar should find support at S$1.5973 and resistance at S$1.6007. \par   The 10-year bond yield slipped four basis points to 3.49%, while the 10-year interest-rate swap rate briefly dropped below 3.90% from a peak of 3.97% Thursday, after a local bank stepped up buying to adjust positions, said a dealer. \par   The gains were unexpected after U.S. Treasurys fell overnight and traded flat in Tokyo trade Friday. \par   The two-year yield slipped one basis point to 3.08%, flattening the curve by three basis points to 41 basis points. \par   The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps. \par                      Latest  Previous \par Overnight Rate       2.813%   2.750% \par Two-year bond yield  3.08%    3.09% \par 10-year bond yield   3.49%    3.53% \par Two-year swap offer  3.54%    3.60% \par 10-year swap offer   3.90%    3.97% \par 2-10-yr swap spread: 36 bps   37 bps \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\uc2 \f2  -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com -Edited by Graham Norris -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060421e24l004eg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
