\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late On Ahead Of U.S. Data, Holidays  \b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 April 2006\par 09:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar gained slightly in Asian trade Thursday due to moderate profit-taking on the U.S. dollar and caution ahead of the release of key U.S. retail sales data.  \par \par \uc2 The government bond yield curve steepened as weaker U.S. Treasuries pushed long-term yields higher while the two-year bond yield rose only modestly, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0600 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6088, below S$1.6120 at the New York close Wednesday. The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6120 in late Asian trade Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the day, investors sold the U.S. dollar, tracking the stronger Japanese yen, on the view it had gone as high as it can go in Asia, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 They said some investors also bought the Singapore dollar as part of cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deals but were unclear on the specifics of such deals.  \par \par \uc2 However, overall caution ahead of the release of U.S. March retail and food sales data later Thursday and the long holiday weekend ahead is expected to temper any sharp fluctuations overnight and keep the U.S. dollar rangebound between its Asian close and upward resistance of S$1.6138, a dealer with a local bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Singapore dollar should remain in a narrow range in the short term with upside potential capped by fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from going outside its policy band.  \par \par \uc2 MAS Deputy Chairman Tharman Shanmugaratnam reportedly said Wednesday there is no need for a faster appreciation of the Singapore dollar over the medium term.  \par \par \uc2 He said the decision by MAS to maintain its current policy was appropriate.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank economist Jimmy Koh said he expects external developments - such as sustained pressure on China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate - to determine the Singapore dollar's course.  \par \par \uc2 He said that in the short term, the U.S. dollar should trade in a range of S$1.600-S$1.6200 on a trade-weighted basis.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the two-year yield climbed one basis point to 3.18% while the 10-year yield rose five basis points to 3.66%, steepening the spread by four basis points to 48 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds have been under selling pressure as weaker U.S. Treasurys pushed long-term yields higher while short-end yields rose on expectations of U.S rate hikes and disappointment with the MAS maintaining its policy of allowing only a modest appreciation in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps. \par                      Latest  Previous \par Overnight Rate       3.00%    2.875% \par Two-year bond yield  3.18%    3.17% \par 10-year bond yield   3.66%    3.61% \par Two-year swap offer  3.68%    3.68% \par 10-year swap offer   4.00%    3.93% \par 2-10-yr swap spread: 32 BPS   25 BPS \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\uc2 \f2  -By Abdul Hadhi, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4153; abdul.hadhi@dowjones.com -Edited by Michael Kitchen -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 D