\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Up Pre-MAS Review, Bond Curve Steepens  \b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2006\par 10:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar strengthened in Asia Monday as the issue of another set of strong economic data reinforced expectations of monetary tightening, market participants said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices fell at the long end, but were flat at the short end, causing the yield curve to steepen, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0600 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6048, below S$1.6073 at the New York close Friday. The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6065 in late Asian trade Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar opened little changed from the New York close in the Singapore session, but soon drifted downward as expectations grew that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore may tighten its policy Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 According to advance estimates issued earlier Monday, Singapore's economy grew 9.1% in the first quarter from a year earlier, its strongest growth rate in two years.  \par \par \uc2 Soon after the data were issued, the U.S. dollar slipped to S$1.6036 before stabilizing as bigger losses were capped by fears that the MAS might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit has been rising of late due to expectations that Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would either allow a one-time upward shift of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band or choose a steeper appreciation path. Last week, the U.S. dollar slid to near an eight-month low of S$1.6016 before recovering on suspected MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current stance is to allow a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar's trade-weighted exchange rate - a policy unchanged since April 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp. said in a note that it expects the MAS to shift the midpoint of the exchange rate upward, which would still leave sufficient room for policy flexibility if global growth slows toward the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 BNP Paribas said the next key support level for the U.S. dollar is at S$1.6000, which, if broken, might push the greenback to S$1.5775.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the yield on the two-year paper was flat, but the 10-year yield climbed two basis points to 3.60%.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer at a local bank said the market wasn't volatile despite selling in U.S. Treasurys Friday because short-end bonds were supported by inflows from foreign funds, which are targeting greater gains for the Singapore dollar after the MAS policy review.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps. \par                      Latest  Previous \par Overnight Rate       2.750%   3.00% \par Two-year bond yield  3.11%    3.11% \par 10-year bond yield   3.60%   3.58% \par Two-year swap offer  3.61%   3.62% \par 10-year swap offer   3.92%   3.90% \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2-10-yr swap spread: 31 BPS 28 BPS Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\uc2 \f2  -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com -Edited by Ven Ram -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060410e24a006k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picsc