\par\par \b \uc2 Sing dlr seen hitting 1.6/dollar on tighter policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2006\par 09:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 10 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar could hit the psychologically important 1.6 per U.S. dollar mark after a likely tightening of monetary policy on Tuesday but further gains may be limited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <SGD=> has strengthened 3.6 percent this year and on Monday rose as high as 1.6035 per dollar, just short of last Wednesday's 1.6020, its highest since June 1998.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts have speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), which controls monetary policy by guiding the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would let the Singapore dollar appreciate at a faster pace to fight off imported inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is pricing a 70-80 percent chance that they will tighten policy. If that happens, we could see a break of 1.6 for a brief while," said a Singapore dollar trader.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll of 13 analysts last week showed that eight expected the MAS would allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise further, while sticking to its two-year policy of a gradual and modest appreciation bias.  \par \par \uc2 The other five economists said MAS would leave its policy unchanged at its meeting on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 A tighter policy could mean either strengthening the mid-point of the MAS's secret trading band for the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or widening the band to allow for sharper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Westpac Banking Corp., said he expected the MAS to strengthen the mid-point of the band by 3 percent to adjust for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise since the last policy meeting in October.  \par \par \uc2 "This does not mean, however, that the MAS will be inviting further sharp appreciation -- it has already seen such a move," Callow said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 "After all, the global growth environment will not look so benign by the October review if the U.S. economy has slowed down."  \par \par \uc2 He expects the MAS to maintain its policy of a gradual and modest rise in the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which he expects would allow for a 0.5 percent appreciation in the next 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 "So, while as of Tuesday theoretically MAS might not feel obliged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support U.S. dollar/\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar until the low 1.57 area, more realistically they will be unhappy about a sudden rapid rise," he said.  \par \par \uc2 IDEAGlobal said in a report that the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be capped at the 1.6 per dollar level for the time being, even if the MAS strengthened the mid-point of its preferred trading range.  \par \par \uc2 That was because a one-off tightening of policy had already been factored into the exchange rate with the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  2.5 percent above the estimated mid-point.  \par \par \uc2 "Thus, the risks are for a 'sell-on-the-fact' after the meeting," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 To profit from a Singapore dollar sell-off, investors could buy the South Korean won against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a target of 0.1700 Singapore cents per won <KRWSGD=R> and then 0.1720, it said. The pair traded at about 0.1682 on Monday. 