\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Gains Capped By MAS Talk; Bonds Weaken  \b0\par\par\uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2006\par 10:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose in Asia Friday, benefiting from a broad rally in key Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but shed some early gains on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices dropped in thin trade as players focused on the release late Friday of U.S. non-farm jobs data that might signal future U.S. interest rate moves, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0650 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6065, below S$1.6074 at the New York close Thursday. The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6059 in late Asian trade Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session the U.S. dollar dipped to S$1.6030 as foreign hedge funds were seen buying the Singapore dollar, the Korean won and the Thai baht, said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, talk emerged in the afternoon session that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping the U.S. dollar to recover from its morning low, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, analysts said interest in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain strong ahead of Chinese President Hu Jintao's summit meeting with U.S. President George W. Bush on April 20.  \par \par \uc2 The market is speculating about a possible Chinese goodwill gesture during the visit, such as allowing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen - which U.S. Congress has been demanding, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit is also benefiting from talk that the MAS may further tighten policy when it announces its semiannual review next Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank said in a report there is a 70% chance the MAS will move the midpoint of the trading band upward as a one-off tightening move to fight rising inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 Philip Wee, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS Bank, said the market doesn't have a consensus on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will change policy. However, he said, the Singapore dollar on a trade-weighted basis has been trading on the stronger side of the band.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar will continue to benefit from broad dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 'If broad dollar is going down, especially against the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dollar-Sing will also move down,' he said  \par \par \uc2 If the MAS tightens monetary policy, it would be either allowing a one-time upward shift of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading band or choosing a steeper appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current stance is to allow a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar's trade-weighted exchange rate - a policy adopted in April 2004.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, yield on the two-year paper rose two basis points to 3.14%, while the 10-year yield climbed one basis point to 3.58%, slightly flattening the curve.  \par \par \uc2 The market was cautious ahead of U.S. non-farm payrolls data later on Friday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps. \par                      Latest  Previous \par Overnight Rate       3.00%   2.87% \par Two-year bond yield  3.14%   3.12% \par 10-year bond yield   3.58%   3.57% \par Two-year swap offer  3.62%   3.62% \par 10-year swap offer   3.90%   3.87% \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 