\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Rises On Foreign Buying; Bonds Stabilize  \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2006\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar gained in Asia Tuesday as foreign investment banks bought the local unit amid weak sentiment for the U.S. unit and in anticipation of possible monetary tightening by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices recovered after heavy selling Monday, as local players stepped in to cover short positions, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0611 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6141, down from S$1.6157 at the close of New York trade Monday. The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6176 in Asian trade late Monday. The market was volatile from the start of session when U.S. investment banks pushed the U.S. dollar down to S$1.6143 before corporate buyers stepped in to buy the greenback, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But the local unit then regained ground following a media report that China could reduce its holding of U.S. debt.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit fell further, dropping to an intraday low of S$1.6136 on news reports that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow may be on his way out.  \par \par \uc2 However fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented the U.S. dollar from falling to its March low of S$1.6122, said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, United Overseas Bank said in a note the U.S. dollar may test S$1.600 this week as speculation grows that MAS may shift to a tighter monetary policy stance.  \par \par \uc2 Economists expect the MAS may either allow a one-time shift upward of its trading band for the Singapore dollar allowing it to drift upward, or simply permit a fast appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against its basket of trading-partner \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, prices stabilized after local players stepped in to cover positions triggered by Monday's across-the-board selling on fears of higher U.S. rates.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year yield slipped by one basis points to 3.24% while the 10-year yield declined by two basis points to 3.60%.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        2.875%     3.06% \par Two-year bond yield   3.24%      3.25% \par 10-year bond yield    3.60%      3.62% \par Two-year swap offer   3.71%      3.71% \par 10-year swap offer    3.95%      3.92% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  24 BPs     21 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Michael Kitchen [ 04-04-06 0643GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060404e24400091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
