\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Weak Before FOMC; Bonds Lower On US Tsys  \b0\par\par\uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 11:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar fell in Asian trade Tuesday, hurt by weakness in the Japanese yen with sentiment cautious ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee's interest rate decision later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices fell at the long end of the yield curve on weaker U.S. Treasurys, but declined at a modest pace at the short end ahead of a two-year bond auction Wednesday, they said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6195, up from S$1.6183 at the close of New York trade Monday. The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6184 in Asian trade late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the U.S. dollar found support in anticipation of another 25-basis-point U.S. rate hike, sending the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.  \par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are looking for clues on future interest-rate moves in the FOMC statement and weren't keen to go long on the U.S. dollar amid uncertainty about U.S. monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 In the short term, the U.S. dollar should trade in a narrow range since it was finding strong support at S$1.6150 and its gains were capped at S$1.6200, said Selene Ling, a treasury economist at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar's downside support was driven by fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the local unit from rising to sharply, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants continued to debate what to expect from an MAS policy review in April.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank, in a note to its clients, said it expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a tight monetary stance because of a strong economy and inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 But the German bank cautioned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't change the current path of modest and gradual appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Sanjeev Sanyal, an economist at Deutsche Bank, said he expects the MAS to maintain what he thinks is a 3% appreciation target that it has pursued in the past year against the trade-weighted basket. The MAS has never disclosed its target.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Deutsche Bank expects the U.S. dollar to slide to S$1.55 over 12 months, he said. \par   JPMorgan said the Singapore dollar stands out as the most likely Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to continue its steady appreciation against U.S. dollar despite the Federal Reserve's rate hike. \par   The U.S. bank expects the MAS to allow a one-off tightening in April, when it recenters the midpoint of the trading band 1.0%-1.5% above the current level to help ward off inflationary pressures. \par   In the bond market, yields on 10-year government bonds rose three basis points to 3.53%, while the two-year yield rose by one basis point to 3.09%. \par   Players were waiting for the FOMC outcome and were cautious ahead of a two-year bond auction Wednesday. \par   OCBC expects the market to bid a 3.1%-3.2% yield in the auction for the S$1 billion of bonds. The current benchmark bond was trading at 3.09%, while the bond that will be reopened Wednesday was trading at 3.15% late Tuesday. \par   The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps: \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        3.06%      3.06% \par Two-year bond yield   3.09%      3.08% \par 10-year bond yield    3.53%      3.50% \par Tw