\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Steady ahead of Fed, firm undertone  \b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 04:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 28 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  barely moved on Tuesday as traders waited for the outcome of a Federal Reserve policy meeting at which U.S. rates were expected to be raised for the 15th time since mid-2004.  \par \par \uc2  Yet, despite the caution, the tone on the Asian regionals remained firm as markets anticipated more gains in the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>, possible policy tightening in Singapore and the likelihood of faster appreciation in the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS>.  \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the yuan hit its highest level since its July revaluation, rising to 8.0205 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a record-high daily mid-point, around which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is allowed to fluctuate in a tight band.  \par \par \uc2  U.S. Senators Charles Schumer and Lindsey Graham, proponents of a bill that threatens China with tariffs on its exports if it does not allow the yuan to rise faster, visited the country last week.  \par \par \uc2  That, and Chinese President Hu Jintao's planned visit to the United States in mid-April, have stoked speculation Beijing will allow faster appreciation.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a rising yuan and Japanese yen <JPY=> would push up \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Singapore dollar <SGD=> that are used as proxies to trade the yuan. Others such as the Korean won <KRW=> that had already raced ahead might not gain so much.  \par \par \uc2  The won fell marginally to near 977 per dollar, weighed down by expectations of seasonal dollar demand as Korean corporations pay dividends to foreigners.  \par \par \uc2  Philip Wee, DBS Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said he expected the won to weaken to about 8.5 per yen from levels around 8.36 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah <IDR=>, in contrast, firmed modestly to a 15-month high of 9,000 a dollar, with traders bracing for technical factors and flows into high-yielding Indonesian bonds to push it to levels around 8,800.  \par \par \uc2  "I am bullish rupiah on the 12-month view. The rupiah has become a policy tool for policy makers," said Lim Su Sian, regional economist with the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2  Lim said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation was a better approach to tackling Indonesia's inflationary pressures, given there was limited room to raise double-digit interest rates further.  \par \par \uc2  SING DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, although the Singapore dollar weakened on demand for U.S. dollars from importers, there was little impact from news that state-owned investment firm Temasek [TEM.UL] was buying a stake in bank Standard Chartered Plc .  \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.6202 per dollar, inching close to Monday's low of 1.6210 and half a cent away from the previous session's highs. Traders rejected the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) to rein in the Sing dollar.  \par \par \uc2  Temasek's announcement on Tuesday that it had agreed to buy an 11.55 percent stake in Standard Chartered, worth approximately $4 billion, was old news, traders said. "That talk was on the streets about 10-12 days ago, when a large sterling-Sing dollar deal had gone through," said one Singapore-based trader.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts maintained their bullishness on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it would gain against the U.S. dollar irrespective of whether the Fed announces another rate rise on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2  JPMorgan strategist Claudio Piron said the Sing dollar was supported by expectations that the MAS would probably move up the centre of the trade-weighted band within which it manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it reviews policy in April.  \par \par \uc2  The MAS conducts monetary policy by managing the Singapore dollar against an undisclosed band based on a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has maintained a stance of gradual and modest appreciation since April 2004.  \par \par \uc2  Lim differed. She said she did not expect the MAS to further tighten policy given inflation was benign and likely to remain so, except for some upward pressure in May when recently announced budgetary hand-outs to low-income groups were made.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0838 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.72         116.71       -0.01\par  Sing dlr        1.6184         1.6172       -0.07\par  Taiwan dlr      32.572         32.570       -0.01\par  Korean won      976.60         975.50       -0.11\par  Baht             38.84          38.92       +0.21\par  Peso             51.12          51.13       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9047.00        9031.00       -0.18\par  Rupee            44.65          44.66       +0.03\par  Ringgit         3.6940         3.6930       -0.03\par  Yuan            8.0213         8.0223       +0.01\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.72         117.96       +1.06\par  Sing dlr        1.6184         1.6625       +2.72\par  Taiwan dlr      32.572         32.850       +0.85\