\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Slips Late Vs USD On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 07:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, which surged to a seven-year high against the U.S. dollar in overnight trades, fell in Asia Friday due to a pullback in the yen and on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds extended recent losses, with the two-year yield surging to its highest level since December 2000 after an overnight selloff in U.S. Treasurys and continued issuance of corporate bonds, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0602 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6210, up from S$1.6178 in New York trade Thursday. In overnight dealings, the U.S. dollar slid to S$1.6128, the lowest level since trading at S$1.6050 on Oct. 16, 1998, but recovered ground in Asian early morning trades.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was at S$1.6187 in Asian trade late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer at a local bank said the U.S. dollar regained strength against the local unit after erasing losses against the yen in early trade.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar had dipped to Y115.91 in New York close, but rebounded to Y116.39 by Friday afternoon, while pushing ahead against other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore won't tolerate swift gains in the Singapore dollar also prevented the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from extending overnight gains, said a second dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the weakness, some banks improved their forecast for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year because of a strong economy and expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may allow a steeper rise in the exchange rate to counter inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs said in a report said that it is revising upward its forecast for the Singapore dollar because of an expansionary budget that will promote economic growth and an expected tightening of monetary policy to attack inflation.  \par \par \uc2 It expects the dollar to slide to S$1.58 in three months, S$1.54 in six months and S$1.50 in 12 months. Its previous forecast for the three periods were S$1.62, S$1.60 and S$1.58.  \par \par \uc2 It also expects the MAS to allow a steeper appreciation of the trade-weighted exchange rate without signaling an actual change in its policy stance.  \par \par \uc2 Calyon Bank said it expects the U.S. dollar to slide to S$1.55 by the end of the year because of an expected tightening in policy and a strong economy.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, weaker U.S. Treasurys and continued issuance of corporate bonds dragged prices lower.  \par \par \uc2 The Housing Development Board has priced a S$100-million, 10-year bond at 3.73%, a week after it sold a S$400 million five-year bond, bankers said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year bond yield climbed seven basis points to 2.97% - its since Dec. 2000. The 10-year bond yield rose five basis points to 3.57%, the highest level for the yield since July 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Selena Ling, a fixed-income analyst at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp., said that bond market sentiment is bearish because players are willing to pay in the interest rate swap market amid concerns that rates might climb further.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight 