 \uc2 Despite the weakness, some banks improved their forecast for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year because of a strong economy and expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may allow a steeper rise in the exchange rate to counter inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs said in a report said that it is revising upward its forecast for the Singapore dollar because of an expansionary budget that will promote economic growth and an expected tightening of monetary policy to attack inflation.  \par \par \uc2 It expects the dollar to slide to S$1.58 in three months, S$1.54 in six months and S$1.50 in 12 months. Its previous forecast for the three periods were S$1.62, S$1.60 and S$1.58.  \par \par \uc2 It also expects the MAS to allow a steeper appreciation of the trade-weighted exchange rate without signaling an actual change in its policy stance.  \par \par \uc2 Calyon Bank said it expects the U.S. dollar to slide to S$1.55 by the end of the year because of an expected tightening in policy and a strong economy.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    In the bond market, weaker U.S. Treasurys and continued issuance of corporate bonds dragged prices lower. \par   The Housing Development Board has priced a S$100-million, 10-year bond at 3.73%, a week after it sold a S$400 million five-year bond, bankers said Friday. \par   The two-year bond yield climbed seven basis points to 2.97% - its since Dec. 2000. The 10-year bond yield rose five basis points to 3.57%, the highest level for the yield since July 2004. \par   Selena Ling, a fixed-income analyst at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp., said that bond market sentiment is bearish because players are willing to pay in the interest rate swap market amid concerns that rates might climb further. \par   The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps: \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        3.063%      